# .punused-ignore — adjudicated punused findings.
#
# The CI gate (.github/workflows/go-ci.yaml) runs punused per directory, then
# matches every finding line against the literal substrings in this file. A
# finding that matches nothing fails the build; an entry that matches nothing
# warns as stale. Full-line "#" comments carry the reason.
#
# punused (github.com/bep/punused v0.5.0) drives gopls and reports two codes:
#   EU1002 "is unused"          — gopls found ZERO references.
#   EU1001 "is used in test only" — every reference is in a _test.go file.
# Its reference view is one Go module with default build tags, so it cannot see:
# cross-language consumers (the generated TypeScript client), callers inside an
# external library that dials back into our interface, reflection dispatch
# (encoding/json, yaml, fmt.Stringer, container/heap), or an interface member
# that is load-bearing only through interface-to-interface assignment. Every
# entry below names which of those applies, verified against the code.
#
# An entry is an adjudication, not an amnesty: adding one means the symbol was
# checked and defended. Real dead code gets deleted instead.

# --- Generated cross-language wire contract: HTTP path constants -------------
# internal/apipaths/paths.gen.go carries "Code generated by wiregen. DO NOT
# EDIT." Every constant is emitted by cmd/wire-codegen from the wirespec
# endpoint table and lands in internal/server/static-src/wire/client.gen.ts as
# `export const PATH_* = "..."`. Verified: all 63 flagged constants' VALUES are
# present there as PATH_* consts, and 12 hand-written TS modules (plus 3 test
# modules) consume PATH_*. On the Go side only the CLI dials these constants
# (cli_remote.go, cli_specs.go — 7 references each); routes.go registers literal
# ServeMux patterns instead, so the unreferenced constants exist for the TS half
# of the contract, which gopls cannot see. The file is regenerated wholesale, so
# it is adjudicated as a unit.
internal/apipaths/paths.gen.go:

# --- Generated cross-language wire contract: ErrorCode catalog ---------------
# internal/api/error_codes.go is the machine-readable error-code catalog. The
# defined type (not an alias) exists so wiregen discovers it as a TS string
# union: every flagged constant's VALUE appears in the generated
# wire/types.gen.ts `ErrorCode` union AND in the hand-written
# static-src/error_codes.ts map, and the file header states clients consume
# these as a contract that must not be renamed. Verified: all 58 values are in
# the generated union, 57 are in the hand-written map, and none of the 23
# flagged constants is absent from both — so none is dead vocabulary.
# NOTE for maintainers: 23 catalog entries have no Go emitter at all, which
# contradicts this file's own "3+ call sites" header rule. That is a pending
# product decision (see the review report), not a licence to grow the catalog.
internal/api/error_codes.go:

# --- Interface members reached only through a narrow interface --------------
# A consumer declares a narrow interface and is handed a value of a wider type,
# so gopls attributes the call to the narrow member and the declaration on the
# other side looks unused. ONE entry is left, and it names its dialer:
#   EmbeddedPolicy  coverage.CountCfg declares it (count.go) and the bound
#                   missing-count closure dials it, but only the tests construct
#                   a CountCfg directly, so gopls sees test-only use.
#
# `method Ping` was removed rather than re-justified. It was listed because
# api.SonarrClient and api.RadarrClient each DECLARED Ping while the connectivity
# check dialled it through confighandlers' anonymous one-method interface, so the
# two nine- and seven-method declarations looked unreferenced. Both are gone: the
# ping surface is now confighandlers.ArrPinger, the check dials Ping through the
# interface that declares it, and the composition root's server.SonarrClient /
# server.RadarrClient embed ArrPinger instead of re-listing Ping. Verified by
# re-running punused over internal/server (which cascades into every handler
# subpackage), internal/server/confighandlers and internal/arrsvc — Ping is
# reported nowhere.
#
# FIVE entries were removed here rather than re-justified, because the thing
# that forced them is gone. MinScoreForTarget, ProvidersForTarget and
# ProviderPriority were listed for `api.ConfigProvider -> search.SearchCfg`, and
# PollInterval for a `var _ PollerCfg = api.ConfigProvider(nil)` assertion that
# checked the wrong direction (that a 28-method interface still carried 5, true
# of any superset). api.ConfigProvider is deleted: every consumer names the
# config surface it calls, *config.Config is handed to those names directly, and
# all four methods are now dialed through the interface that declares them.
# Verified by re-running punused on internal/search, internal/server/polling and
# internal/config — none of the four is reported any more.
#
# The two STORE members that used to sit here went the same way when api.Store
# was deleted.
method EmbeddedPolicy

# --- Concrete implementations reached only through their interface -----------
# The mirror image of the block above: production dials these through an
# interface, so gopls attributes the reference to the interface member and the
# implementation looks unused.
#   (*Config).EmbeddedPolicy   Dialed through the FOUR narrow interfaces that
#                              declare it -- queryhandlers' queryCfg,
#                              coveragehandlers' coverageCfg, coverage.CountCfg
#                              and search.SearchCfg -- each of which is handed a
#                              *config.Config by name at the composition root
#                              (server_init.go, main.go's search.WithConfig).
#                              Those typed parameters ARE the compile-time check
#                              that used to be a `var _ api.ConfigProvider`
#                              assertion in config_load.go: removing the method
#                              breaks every one of the four assignments.
#   (*Config).Validate         config_types.go var _ Validator. NOTE for
#                              maintainers: this is the only reference. Nothing
#                              in production calls Validate -- LoadFromBytes
#                              calls the package-level validate(ctx, cfg)
#                              directly -- and config.Validator's only consumer
#                              is the test that asserts the two agree. The
#                              interface, the method and that test form a closed
#                              loop; whether the type should carry a
#                              self-validation method at all is a pending
#                              decision, not a licence to keep it.
#
# (*NopConfig).EmbeddedPolicy was removed: internal/testsupport/nopconfig.go is
# deleted. It existed to satisfy api.ConfigProvider whole, and the seven
# packages that shared its 28 no-op methods now each carry a fixture sized to
# the interface their own subject names.
method (*Config).EmbeddedPolicy
method (*Config).Validate

# --- External-library store contract (cplieger/auth/v4) ---------------------
# internal/authstore/authdb.go:27-31 asserts *Store against the auth library's
# persistence SPI as FIVE separate role interfaces — auth.UserStore,
# auth.SessionPersister, auth.PasskeyStore, auth.KeyStore, auth.OIDCStateStore —
# so a failure names the role that broke. v4 merged the store SPI into the auth
# root package, so there is no `store.Composite` and no `AuthStore` alias here
# any more; the union subflux does declare is server.AuthStore, at the one site
# that fans a single concrete store out to five consumers.
# All ten methods below are members of those roles. Their callers are the auth
# library's own authenticator, session, passkey, and API-key paths — a different
# module, which is exactly what punused documents it cannot see. Removing any one
# breaks an assertion at compile time (verified for UserCount).
#
# This header was re-verified against v4 on 2026-08-19. It previously cited
# auth/v2, a `store.Composite` alias, `authdb.go:16` (which is the `"time"`
# import) and "the resolved auth/v2@v2.1.1 store package", a version that no
# longer resolves. The methods were still correctly adjudicated; only the
# evidence had rotted, which this file's own header calls the difference between
# an adjudication and an amnesty.
method (*Store).GetUserByID
method (*Store).GetUserByEmail
method (*Store).UserCount
method (*Store).CreateSession
method (*Store).GetSessionByHash
method (*Store).UpdateSessionActivity
method (*Store).DeleteSession
method (*Store).GetPasskeyByCredentialID
method (*Store).UpdatePasskeyAfterLogin
method (*Store).GetAPIKeyByHash

# The same library callback seen from the CONSUMER side: authhandlers.AccountStore
# declares UpdatePasskeyAfterLogin (iface.go:30) because auth's CompleteLogin
# performs that credential-custody write through the store it is handed. Nothing
# in subflux calls it directly, and dropping it from the interface would silently
# stop the sign-count and flag persistence a passkey replay check depends on.
method UpdatePasskeyAfterLogin

